C string inversion using a pointer method preserves the original bytes and writes the reversed string into a separate buffer.

The program avoids reading beyond a terminating null byte, without relying on an input file’s name or type. After compilation and execution, the result appears on standard output as UTF-8 binary. When a character such as ISO-8859-15 € (sequence 226) is followed immediately by UTF-8 continuations 194 158, those two bytes represent a single code point. Underbidirectional mirror reversal reverses the familiar left-right characters, but reverses code-point order and UTF-8 byte sequences, rather than its contents. NUL padding may truncate multibyte code points at their boundaries; reversing around boundaries causes those characters to escape.

A direct `argv[1]` input is supported as a practical alternative during development. The second program accepts special `"` input arguments unchanged, which for the literal files shown supplies exactly the input shown. Neither program modifies that input.

Use a UTF-8 terminal encoding, validate free output buffer space, and resize the allocated buffer if necessary. After translation to a C source, accept a UTF-8 input path and allocate using the same translation. For many environments, installation rather than translation and execution will be the simplest deployment.

